// helpLists asserts the help output captured by `help` advertises the
// command named in the step text.
func helpLists(state *State, args []string) error {
 if len(args) == 0 {
  return errors.New(`the help lists <command>: step matched but captured no command name`)
 }
 // The pattern has exactly one capture group, so its value is the last
 // element whether or not the harness prepends the full match.
 command := strings.TrimSpace(args[len(args)-1])
 if command == "" {
  return errors.New(`the help lists <command>: captured command name is empty`)
 }
 if state.Help == "" {
  return fmt.Errorf("expected the engine help to list %q, but no help output was captured: request the engine help before asserting on it", command)
 }
 if !strings.Contains(state.Help, command) {
  return fmt.Errorf("expected the engine help to list %q, but it is absent from the help output; got:\n%s", command, state.Help)
 }
 return nil
}
```

## Why the implementation looks like this

- **Reuses the suite's state type.** It reads `state.Help`, the field the
  existing `help` When step populates from `<EngineBin> --help`. It does not
  re-run the engine — re-invoking `spec.Run` here would duplicate what the
  sibling definition already does, which is exactly the drift to avoid.
- **Then asserts and names both sides on failure.** Each error states what
  was expected (the captured command) and what happened (no help captured,
  or the full help output that lacked it), because that error text IS the
  failure a reader gets from `go test`.
- **Distinguishes "no help yet" from "help lacks the command."** An empty
  `state.Help` means the When step never ran or produced nothing; reporting
  that separately stops a missing-command report that is really a
  missing-invocation report.
- **Capture access is index-convention agnostic.** `args[len(args)-1)]` →
  written as `args[len(args)-1]` resolves to the capture group under both
  the "submatches only" and "full match at index 0" conventions, so the fix
  does not hinge on an unverifiable detail of
  `github.com/ondatra-ai/true-bdd/pkg/testkit/bddgo`.

